  {
   "type": "done",
   "answer": "No, DDR5's two 32-bit subchannels do **not** mean that the total data width doubles.\n\nAccording to the lecture, \"DDR5 splits 64 data bits into two 32-bit subchannels. This split alone does not double total data width\" [S1, p. 40]. The total data width remains 64 bits; the split is simply a division of that existing width into two independent 32-bit channels to improve concurrency and bus utilization, not an increase in the total number of data bits transferred per clock cycle.\n\n**Source:**\n- [S1] Page 40: \"DDR5 splits 64 data bits into two 32-bit subchannels. This split alone does not double total data width.\"",
   "citations": [
    {
     "document_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402",
     "chunk_id": "ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402-p40-1",
     "page": 40,
     "text": "CPU Memory Hierarchy\n\nDRAM Behind Cache: Channel Bandwidth Grows Along Three\nAxes\n\n\n\n\n\n  Independent channels add bandwidth when requests use them concurrently. Bank\n▪\n  scheduling improves bus utilization.\n DDR5 splits 64 data bits into two 32-bit subchannels. This split alone does not\n▪\n  double total data width.\n\nMemory Wall     CPU Memory    GPU Memory    SOTA Memory     Advanced AI Computing and Systems        40",
     "preview_url": "/api/documents/ad66f2c8360da43ea0981bffa549956185949044062a0abe4689381ced423402/pages/40/preview",
     "source": "S1"
    }
